{"cuisine/kerala-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/kerala-cuisine","n":"Kerala cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["South Indian cuisine","cuisine/south-indian-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in India","intro":[["Kerala cuisine is a culinary style that originated in Kerala, a state on the southwestern Malabar Coast of India. It includes both vegetarian and non-vegetarian dishes prepared using fish, poultry and red meat, with rice as a typical accompaniment. Chillies, curry leaves, coconut, mustard seeds, turmeric, tamarind, asafoetida and other spices are also used in the preparation."],["Kerala is known as the \"Land of Spices\" because it traded spices with Europe as well as with many ancient civilizations, with the oldest historical records of the Sumerians from 3000 BCE."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"kerala-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kerala_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"Yummy Tummy Aarthi (South Indian)","ref":"site/yummytummyaarthi-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"yummytummyaarthi.com","note":"Kerala section of Aarthi's large South Indian home cooking blog.","url":"https://yummytummyaarthi.com/recipes/kerala","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"The Kerala Kitchen","ref":"cookbook/the-kerala-kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Lathika George","note":"Recipes and food memories from Kerala, published by Hippocrene.","url":"https://www.hippocrenebooks.com/store/p497/TheKeralaKitchen.html","suggested":true},{"name":"Coconut Lagoon: Recipes from a South Indian Kitchen","ref":"cookbook/coconut-lagoon-recipes-from-a-south-indian-kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Joe Thottungal","note":"Kerala dishes from the Ottawa restaurant Coconut Lagoon, published by Figure 1.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":3,"links":[["Chutney","dish/chutney"],["Pundi","dish/pundi"],["South Indian parotta","dish/south-indian-parotta"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Coconut","ingredient/coconut"],["Sodium chloride","ingredient/sodium-chloride"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Maida (flour)","ingredient/maida-flour"],["Wheat flour","ingredient/wheat-flour"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Part of","links":[["South Indian cuisine","cuisine/south-indian-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/muhajir-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/muhajir-cuisine","n":"Muhajir cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Pakistan","intro":[["Karachi cuisine refers to the cuisine found mainly in the city of Karachi, Sindh, Pakistan. It is a multicultural cuisine as a result of the city consisting of various ethnic groups from different parts of Pakistan. Karachi is considered the melting pot of Pakistan."],["The cuisine of Karachi is strongly influenced by the city's Muhajir population, who came from various parts of British India and settled primarily in Karachi after the independence of Pakistan in 1947. Most Urdu speaking Muslims have traditionally been based in Karachi, hence the city is known for multi cultural tastes in its cuisine. These Muslims maintained their old established culinary traditions, including variety of dishes and beverages."],["Karachi cuisine is renowned for its cultural fusion, due to various empires and peoples living in this mega city. As a result many multi ethnic cuisines collaboratively had an influence on the style of Karachi food. The Pakistani cuisines such as Sindhi cuisine, Punjabi cuisine, Pashtun cuisine,  Kalash cuisine, Saraiki cuisine,  Kashmiri cuisine,  Balochi cuisine, Chitrali cuisine and other regional cuisines have also influenced the cuisine of Karachi."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"muhajir-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Muhajir_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":1,"rows":[{"name":"The Karachi Kitchen: Classic and Contemporary Flavors of Pakistan","ref":"cookbook/the-karachi-kitchen-classic-and-contemporary-flavors-of-pakistan","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Kausar Ahmed, 2017","note":"Home cooking from Karachi, the center of Muhajir food culture.","url":"","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Part of","links":[["Pakistani cuisine","cuisine/pakistani-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine"},"cuisine/thai-cuisine":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/thai-cuisine","n":"Thai cuisine","c":"#f0b340","crumb":[["Asia","cuisines#asia"],["Cuisine"]],"sub":"National cuisine of Thailand","intro":[["Thai cuisine, the national cuisine of Thailand and of Thai people, places emphasis on lightly prepared dishes with aromatics and spicy heat. Traditional Thai cuisine loosely falls into four categories: tom (Thai: ต้ม, boiled dishes), yam (Thai: ยำ, spicy salads), tam (Thai: ตำ, pounded foods), and kaeng (Thai: แกง, curries). Deep-frying, stir-frying and steaming are methods introduced from Chinese cuisine."],["In 2011, seven Thai dishes appeared on a list of the \"World's 50 Best Foods\", an online poll of 35,000 people worldwide by CNN Travel. Thailand had more dishes on the list than any other country: tom yum kung (4th), pad thai (5th), som tam (6th), massaman curry (10th), green curry (19th), Thai fried rice (24th) and nam tok mu (36th). Thai cuisine is defined by its characteristic balance of sweet, sour, salty, and spicy flavors using fresh ingredients, aromatic herbs, and techniques like stir frying, steaming and deep frying."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"thai-cuisine"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thai_cuisine"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","count":2,"rows":[{"name":"Hot Thai Kitchen","ref":"site/hot-thai-kitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"hot-thai-kitchen.com","note":"Pailin Chongchitnant's recipes and videos, with background on technique and ingredients.","url":"https://hot-thai-kitchen.com","suggested":true},{"name":"SheSimmers","ref":"site/shesimmers-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"shesimmers.com","note":"Bangkok-born writer Leela Punyaratabandhu's blog on Thai home cooking.","url":"https://shesimmers.com/who-is-shesimmers","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Cookbooks","count":3,"rows":[{"name":"Thai Food","ref":"cookbook/thai-food","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"David Thompson, 2002","note":"Encyclopedic reference on Thai cooking and its history.","url":"https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/198298/thai-food-by-david-thompson/9781580084628/","suggested":true},{"name":"Hot Thai Kitchen","ref":"cookbook/hot-thai-kitchen","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Pailin Chongchitnant, 2016","note":"Companion book to the YouTube channel, explaining Thai flavor building.","url":"https://penguinrandomhouse.com/books/258501/hot-thai-kitchen-by-pailin-chongchitnant","suggested":true},{"name":"Simple Thai Food","ref":"cookbook/simple-thai-food","color":"#f0b340","shape":"book","sub":"Leela Punyaratabandhu, 2014","note":"Classic dishes from the Thai home kitchen by the SheSimmers author.","url":"https://openlibrary.org/isbn/9781607745235","suggested":true}]},{"h":"Dishes","count":36,"links":[["Akhuni","dish/akhuni"],["American fried rice","dish/american-fried-rice"],["Boat noodles","dish/boat-noodles"],["Cholada Thai","dish/cholada-thai"],["Drunken noodles","dish/drunken-noodles"],["E-san Thai Cuisine","dish/e-san-thai-cuisine"],["Evil jungle prince","dish/evil-jungle-prince"],["Fish sauce","dish/fish-sauce"],["Green curry","dish/green-curry"],["Hainanese chicken rice","dish/hainanese-chicken-rice"],["Jay Fai","dish/jay-fai"],["Khanom babin","dish/khanom-babin"],["Khanom chin","dish/khanom-chin"],["Khanom thuai","dish/khanom-thuai"],["Khao soi","dish/khao-soi"],["Kiin Kiin","dish/kiin-kiin"],["La tiang","dish/la-tiang"],["Lotus of Siam","dish/lotus-of-siam"],["Mi krop","dish/mi-krop"],["Miang kham","dish/miang-kham"],["Nam ngiao","dish/nam-ngiao"],["Nam tok (food)","dish/nam-tok-food"],["PaaDee","dish/paadee"],["Pad see ew","dish/pad-see-ew"],["Pad thai","dish/pad-thai"],["Rad naa","dish/rad-naa"],["Rice noodle roll","dish/rice-noodle-roll"],["Sorn (restaurant)","dish/sorn-restaurant"],["Sweet chili sauce","dish/sweet-chili-sauce"],["Thai curry","dish/thai-curry"],["Thai Express","dish/thai-express"],["Thai fried rice","dish/thai-fried-rice"],["Thali","dish/thali"],["Thua nao","dish/thua-nao"],["Tom kha kai","dish/tom-kha-kai"],["Tom yum","dish/tom-yum"]]},{"h":"Key ingredients","links":[["Coconut milk","ingredient/coconut-milk"],["Rice","ingredient/rice"],["Rice noodles","ingredient/rice-noodles"],["Chicken","ingredient/chicken"],["Fish sauce","ingredient/fish-sauce"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Tofu","ingredient/tofu"],["Pork","ingredient/pork"],["Garlic","ingredient/garlic"],["Shahe fen","ingredient/shahe-fen"],["Sugar","ingredient/sugar"],["Kaffir lime","ingredient/kaffir-lime"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Galangal","ingredient/galangal"],["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Soybean","ingredient/soybean"],["Bean sprouts","ingredient/bean-sprouts"],["Shrimp","ingredient/shrimp"],["Soy sauce","ingredient/soy-sauce"],["Rice flour","ingredient/rice-flour"],["Gai lan","ingredient/gai-lan"],["Noodles","ingredient/noodles"],["Lime (fruit)","ingredient/lime-fruit"],["Lemongrass","ingredient/lemongrass"]],"note":"Most used across its dishes."},{"h":"Within Thai cooking","count":5,"links":[["Lanna cuisine","cuisine/lanna-cuisine"],["Lao cuisine","cuisine/lao-cuisine"],["Shan cuisine","cuisine/shan-cuisine"],["Southern Thai cuisine","cuisine/southern-thai-cuisine"],["Thai-Chinese cuisine","cuisine/thai-chinese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"More from Thailand","count":3,"links":[["Karen cuisine","cuisine/karen-cuisine"],["Mon cuisine","cuisine/mon-cuisine"],["Thai Chinese cuisine","cuisine/thai-chinese-cuisine"]]}],"cont":"Asian cuisine","img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/4/4b/Lotus_of_siam_facade.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","w":500,"h":375,"by":"Krista","lic":"CC BY 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lotus_of_siam_facade.jpg","of":"Lotus of Siam"}},"cuisine/cuisine-of-hamburg":{"t":"cuisine","r":"cuisine/cuisine-of-hamburg","n":"Hamburg cuisine","c":"#cf5f87","crumb":[["Europe","cuisines#europe"],["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisine"]],"sub":"Regional cuisine in Germany","intro":[["Due to its centuries-old history as a major port town the cuisine of Hamburg is very diversified and sapid as ingredients' supply was safe."],["Until the 20th century, the cuisine of Hamburg was predominantly characterized by the extensive choice of different kinds of fish from the river Elbe and the nearby Baltic Sea. The region of Vierlande supplied Hamburg with fresh vegetables. Fruit came from the area Altes Land and until industrialization the neighbourhood of Wilhelmsburg was considered the ‘milk isle’ of Hamburg. International trade in the Port of Hamburg made spices and exotic nutrition items from India and South America available since the 16th century, which were soon incorporated into civic kitchens."],["On this basis, the cuisine of Hamburg developed which regrettably lost some of its characteristics nowadays due to the supraregional harmonization of the North German cuisine. But due to its high economic importance, Hamburg does feature many internationally recognized gourmet restaurants from which 11 were repeatedly awarded with a Michelin star in 2010."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"cuisine-of-hamburg"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuisine_of_Hamburg"}],"sections":[{"h":"Recipe sites","empty":"None yet","tryLinks":[["German cuisine","cuisine/german-cuisi