,"dish/beurre-maitre-d-hotel"],["Beurre manié","dish/beurre-manie"],["Bisque (food)","dish/bisque-food"],["Bistro 990","dish/bistro-990"],["Bistro Agnes","dish/bistro-agnes"],["Bistro Jeanty","dish/bistro-jeanty"]]}]},"dish/serradura":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/serradura","n":"Serradura","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Portuguese cuisine","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Serradura, also known as sawdust pudding or Macau pudding, is a Portuguese dessert with a layered appearance alternating between whipped cream and crumbled Marie biscuit. The name serradura is Portuguese for \"sawdust\", which refers to the appearance of the finely crushed biscuit crumbs in the pudding. The dessert is popular in Portugal and its former colonies – Macau in China, and Goa in India."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-serradura"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serradura"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Portuguese cuisine","cuisine/portuguese-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":6,"links":[["Biscuit","ingredient/biscuit"],["Condensed milk","ingredient/condensed-milk"],["Cream","ingredient/cream"],["Marie biscuit","ingredient/marie-biscuit"],["Vanilla extract","ingredient/vanilla-extract"],["Whipped cream","ingredient/whipped-cream"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Food From Portugal","ref":"site/foodfromportugal-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"foodfromportugal.com","note":"Portuguese-run recipe site and video channel of traditional Portuguese dishes.","url":"https://www.foodfromportugal.com/","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Portuguese cuisine","links":[["Adega","dish/adega"],["Alheira","dish/alheira"],["Amêijoas à Bulhão Pato","dish/ameijoas-a-bulhao-pato"],["Arroz de marisco","dish/arroz-de-marisco"],["Bacalhau à Brás","dish/bacalhau-a-bras"],["Bacalhau à Gomes de Sá","dish/bacalhau-a-gomes-de-sa"],["Bacalhau à minhota","dish/bacalhau-a-minhota"],["Bacalhau à Narcisa","dish/bacalhau-a-narcisa"],["Bacalhau à Zé do Pipo","dish/bacalhau-a-ze-do-pipo"],["Bacalhau com natas","dish/bacalhau-com-natas"],["Batatas a murro","dish/batatas-a-murro"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Bolo de bolacha","dish/bolo-de-bolacha"],["Canja de galinha","dish/canja-de-galinha"],["Cocido","dish/cocido"],["Embutido","dish/embutido"],["Encharcada","dish/encharcada"],["Fartura","dish/fartura"],["Feijoada","dish/feijoada"],["Fios de ovos","dish/fios-de-ovos"],["Francesinha","dish/francesinha"],["Gazpacho","dish/gazpacho"],["Pão Alentejano","dish/pao-alentejano"],["Pão de Ló","dish/pao-de-lo"],["Pataniscas","dish/pataniscas"],["Polvo à lagareiro","dish/polvo-a-lagareiro"],["Prego (Portuguese sandwich)","dish/prego-portuguese-sandwich"],["Pudim Abade de Priscos","dish/pudim-abade-de-priscos"],["Pudim Molotov","dish/pudim-molotov"],["Sericaia","dish/sericaia"],["Tocino de cielo","dish/tocino-de-cielo"]]}]},"dish/sorrel-soup":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/sorrel-soup","n":"Sorrel soup","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish found in 5 cuisines","dot":"#cf5f87","intro":[["Sorrel soup is made from water or broth, sorrel leaves, and salt. Varieties of the same soup include spinach, garden orache, chard, nettle, and occasionally dandelion, goutweed or ramsons, together with or instead of sorrel. It is known in Ashkenazi Jewish, Belarusian, Estonian, Hungarian, Latvian, Lithuanian, Romanian, Armenian, Georgian, Polish, Russian and Ukrainian cuisines. Its other English names, spelled variously schav, shchav, shav, or shtshav, are borrowed from the Yiddish language, which in turn derives from Slavic languages, like for example Belarusian шчаўе, Russian and Ukrainian щавель, shchavel, Polish szczaw. The soup name comes ultimately from the Proto-Slavic ščаvĭ for sorrel. Due to its commonness as a soup in Eastern European cuisines, it is often called green borscht, as a cousin of the standard, reddish-purple beetroot borscht. In Russia, where shchi (along with or"]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-sorrel-soup"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sorrel_soup"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Belarusian cuisine","cuisine/belarusian-cuisine"],["Jewish cuisine","cuisine/jewish-cuisine"],["Polish cuisine","cuisine/polish-cuisine"],["Russian cuisine","cuisine/russian-cuisine"],["Ukrainian cuisine","cuisine/ukrainian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":8,"links":[["Broth","ingredient/broth"],["Carrot","ingredient/carrot"],["Edible salt","ingredient/edible-salt"],["Egg","ingredient/egg"],["Pork ribs","ingredient/pork-ribs"],["Potato","ingredient/potato"],["Smetana","ingredient/smetana"],["Sorrel","ingredient/sorrel"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Tori Avey: Jewish Recipes","ref":"site/toriavey-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"toriavey.com","note":"Tori Avey's Jewish recipe index, often paired with notes on each dish's history.","url":"https://toriavey.com/jewish-recipes/","suggested":true},{"name":"The Nosher (My Jewish Learning)","ref":"site/myjewishlearning-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"myjewishlearning.com","note":"Recipe section of the nonprofit My Jewish Learning, covering Ashkenazi, Sephardi and Mizrahi home cooking and holiday food.","url":"https://www.myjewishlearning.com/recipes/","suggested":true},{"name":"Polonist","ref":"site/polonist-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"polonist.com","note":"Polish recipes and food culture from a Polish-born writer living abroad.","url":"https://www.polonist.com/about/","suggested":true},{"name":"Polish Your Kitchen","ref":"site/polishyourkitchen-com","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"polishyourkitchen.com","note":"Long-running blog of traditional Polish home recipes.","url":"https://polishyourkitchen.com","suggested":true}]},{"h":"More from Belarusian cuisine","links":[["Angel wings","dish/angel-wings"],["Aspic","dish/aspic"],["Baba (cake)","dish/baba-cake"],["Borscht","dish/